WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.050
Does our agent work?

00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:04.580
We want to know before our users tell us.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.814
Two kinds of checks. The outcome, or the behaviour?

00:00:10.014 --> 00:00:13.945
End-to-end checks ask: did the final answer come out right?

00:00:13.995 --> 00:00:16.044
The score moves, but not why.

00:00:16.144 --> 00:00:19.988
Behavioural checks ask whether one specific thing happened.

00:00:20.038 --> 00:00:22.438
Narrow, binary, and cheap.

00:00:22.988 --> 00:00:27.578
Behavioural checks run on the trace. The trajectory is the truth.

00:00:28.128 --> 00:00:32.639
End-to-end grading needs a judge, and no instrument ships calibrated.

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:38.070
First, agreement with hand labels. Second, repeat-run variance.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:43.009
A golden set must include bad examples, not just good ones.

00:00:43.059 --> 00:00:46.094
Sample real runs on a schedule, grade them,

00:00:46.144 --> 00:00:48.754
and promote anything that fails into the set.

00:00:49.304 --> 00:00:56.614
The practice is five steps. Mine twenty real failures. Write one check per case.

00:00:56.664 --> 00:01:02.462
Run them on traces. Calibrate one narrow judge. Then hand-grade weekly.

00:01:03.012 --> 00:01:08.610
That's module five, evals. Start with page one: Two kinds of checks.
